{"experiments":[
  {
    "hypothesis":"Melanoma patients with elevated lactate_dehydrogenase_result exhibit upregulation of glycolysis pathway genes compared to patients with normal LDH.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test whether elevated serum LDH levels are associated with increased expression of core glycolysis genes in melanoma tumors.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.\n2. Subset clinical data to patients with lactate_dehydrogenase_result == 'Elevated' or 'Normal'.\n3. In the expression matrix, extract the log2(FPKM+0.001) values for key glycolysis genes: ALDOA, ENO1, PKM, LDHA, HK2 (map Ensembl IDs to symbols similarly to previous experiments).\n4. Aggregate sample‐level expression to patient‐level by averaging across samples.\n5. Merge patient‐level expression of glycolysis genes with LDH status.\n6. For each glycolysis gene, compare expression between Elevated vs. Normal groups using Welch’s t‐test and adjust p‐values with Benjamini–Hochberg FDR.\n7. Perform a pathway‐level test by computing a glycolysis signature score (mean of the five genes) per patient and compare signature scores via Mann–Whitney U test.\n8. Visualize results with boxplots for individual genes and the signature score, annotated with p‐values.",
      "deliverables":"• Table of group means, fold‐changes, raw and FDR‐adjusted p‐values for each glycolysis gene.\n• Mann–Whitney U statistic and p‐value for the glycolysis signature score.\n• Boxplots of expression for each gene and the signature score stratified by LDH status."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Patients with higher missense mutation burden exhibit elevated expression of cytotoxic T‐cell markers CD8A and GZMB.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Assess the relationship between tumor missense mutation burden and expression of T‐cell cytotoxicity markers.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.\n2. Filter the mutation data to include only Variant_Classification=='Missense_Mutation'.\n3. Compute missense burden per patient by counting missense mutations, matching patient_id (first 12 characters).\n4. In the expression data, extract CD8A and GZMB, aggregate sample‐level to patient‐level by averaging.\n5. Merge missense burden with expression of CD8A and GZMB.\n6. Compute Spearman correlation between missense burden and each gene’s expression; test significance.\n7. Create scatter plots with regression line for burden vs. expression; annotate with Spearman’s rho and p‐value.",
      "deliverables":"• Spearman correlation coefficients and p‐values for missense burden vs. CD8A and GZMB.\n• Scatter plots with regression lines and annotations.\n• Summary table of patient missense burden distribution."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Body mass index (BMI) is inversely correlated with tumor mutational burden in melanoma patients.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test whether higher BMI at diagnosis is associated with lower tumor mutational burden (TMB).",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_mutations.csv.\n2. From clinical data, calculate BMI = weight_kg / (height_cm/100)^2; exclude missing height or weight.\n3. Compute TMB per patient as total mutations/patient from cleaned_mutations.csv.\n4. Merge BMI and TMB by patient_id.\n5. Compute Pearson and Spearman correlations between BMI and TMB; test significance.\n6. Categorize patients into BMI groups (normal <25, overweight 25–30, obese >30) and compare TMB distributions across groups using Kruskal‐Wallis test.\n7. Visualize with scatter plot (BMI vs. TMB) and boxplots of TMB by BMI category.",
      "deliverables":"• Correlation coefficients (Pearson, Spearman) and p‐values between BMI and TMB.\n• Kruskal‐Wallis test statistic and p‐value for BMI categories.\n• Scatter plot and boxplot visualizations."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Patients who received prior systemic therapy exhibit a higher proportion of non‐synonymous (missense + nonsense) mutations relative to silent mutations than therapy‐naïve patients.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Determine if prior systemic therapy is associated with an altered spectrum of mutation types.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_mutations.csv.\n2. Annotate each patient with prior_systemic_therapy status ('YES' vs. 'NO').\n3. In the mutation data, for each patient, count non‐synonymous mutations (Variant_Classification in ['Missense_Mutation','Nonsense_Mutation','Splice_Site']) and silent mutations (Variant_Classification=='Silent').\n4. Build a 2×2 contingency table: rows=therapy status (YES/NO), columns=mutation type (non‐syn vs. silent).\n5. Perform a chi‐square test of independence on the contingency table.\n6. Report odds ratio for non‐synonymous vs. silent mutations in treated vs. untreated patients.\n7. Create a stacked bar chart showing mutation type proportions by therapy status.",
      "deliverables":"• Contingency table and chi‐square test statistic with p‐value.\n• Odds ratio estimate with confidence interval.\n• Stacked bar chart of mutation type proportions."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Patients diagnosed before age 50 have lower overall TMB but higher expression of the proliferation marker MKI67 compared to patients diagnosed at or after age 50.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Compare TMB and MKI67 expression between younger and older melanoma patients.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv, cleaned_mutations.csv, and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.\n2. Categorize patients by age_at_diagnosis: <50 vs. ≥50.\n3. Compute TMB per patient (total mutation count).\n4. Extract MKI67 expression from the gene expression matrix, aggregate to patient level.\n5. Merge age group, TMB, and MKI67 expression by patient_id.\n6. Compare TMB between age groups using Mann–Whitney U test.\n7. Compare MKI67 expression between age groups using Welch’s t‐test.\n8. Visualize with boxplots for TMB and MKI67 by age group.",
      "deliverables":"• Mann–Whitney U statistic and p‐value for TMB comparison.\n• t‐test statistic and p‐value for MKI67 expression.\n• Boxplots of TMB and MKI67 by age group."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Ulceration-positive tumors display higher expression of angiogenesis‐related genes VEGFA and ANGPT2 compared to non‐ulcerated tumors.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Test whether histologic ulceration in melanoma is associated with upregulated angiogenesis gene expression.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.\n2. Subset clinical data to patients with ulceration_indicator == 'YES' or 'NO'.\n3. In the expression matrix, extract VEGFA and ANGPT2 (map Ensembl IDs to gene symbols).\n4. Aggregate sample‐level expression to patient level.\n5. Merge expression with ulceration status.\n6. For each gene, perform Welch’s t‐test comparing ulcerated vs. non‐ulcerated groups, adjust p‐values by FDR.\n7. Plot boxplots of VEGFA and ANGPT2 expression by ulceration status, annotate with p‐values.",
      "deliverables":"• Table of group means, fold‐changes, raw and FDR‐adjusted p‐values for VEGFA and ANGPT2.\n• Boxplots of expression by ulceration status."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Clark invasion level IV–V tumors have higher expression of matrix metalloproteinases MMP1 and MMP9 than level I–III tumors.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Assess whether deeper Clark levels correlate with upregulation of invasion‐associated MMP genes.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.\n2. Categorize patients by clark_level: group A = levels I–III, group B = levels IV–V; exclude missing.\n3. Extract MMP1 and MMP9 expression from the expression matrix; aggregate to patient level.\n4. Merge expression with Clark group.\n5. Compare expression of each MMP between groups using Mann–Whitney U test; adjust p‐values by FDR.\n6. Visualize with boxplots and annotate p‐values.",
      "deliverables":"• Mann–Whitney U statistics and FDR‐adjusted p‐values for MMP1 and MMP9.\n• Boxplots of MMP1 and MMP9 expression by Clark level group."
    }
  },
  {
    "hypothesis":"Melanoma patients with TP53 mutations have a higher prevalence of elevated LDH and increased CD274 (PD‐L1) expression compared to TP53 wild‐type patients.",
    "experiment_plan":{
      "objective":"Investigate the relationship between TP53 mutation status, LDH elevation, and PD‐L1 expression.",
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv, cleaned_mutations.csv, and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.\n2. Label patients as TP53‐mutant if any Hugo_Symbol=='TP53'; others as wild‐type.\n3. From clinical data, extract lactate_dehydrogenase_result category ('Elevated' vs. 'Normal'); build a 2×2 contingency table of TP53 status vs. LDH status; perform a chi‐square test.\n4. In the expression data, extract CD274 (PD‐L1) expression and aggregate to patient level.\n5. Merge CD274 expression with TP53 status.\n6. Compare CD274 expression between mutant and wild‐type using Welch’s t‐test and adjust for multiple testing if including additional genes.\n7. Visualize with a bar chart of LDH prevalence by TP53 status and a boxplot of CD274 expression by TP53 status.",
      "deliverables":"• Contingency table and chi‐square test statistic with p‐value for TP53 vs. LDH.\n• Mean CD274 expression by TP53 status, t‐test statistic and p‐value.\n• Bar chart of LDH elevation rates and boxplot of PD‐L1 expression by TP53 status."
    }
  }
]}
